= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Choose versions
$$
p_k=\mathbb E[\mathbf1_{B_k}\mid\mathcal F_{k-1}]\in[0,1],\qquad A_0=0,\qquad A_n=\sum_{k=1}^np_k.
$$
The <stochastic process> $(A_n)$ is increasing, and its $k$th increment is $\mathcal F_{k-1}$-<measurable>, so it is <predictable>. The <Doob decomposition theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{X_n=M_n+A_n,\qquad M_0=0,\qquad M_n=\sum_{k=1}^n(\mathbf1_{B_k}-p_k).}
$$
To check the <martingale> property directly, each summand is <integrable> and has zero <conditional expectation> given $\mathcal F_{k-1}$. The sums are an <adapted process> for the given <filtration>. Thus $\mathbb E[M_n\mid\mathcal F_{n-1}]=M_{n-1}$. Its <bounded increments> satisfy $|M_n-M_{n-1}|\leq1$.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Both $X_n$ and $A_n$ are increasing, so each has a <limit of a sequence> in $[0,\infty]$. Apply part (c)(ii) to the <martingale> $M_n=X_n-A_n$, whose <bounded increments> were verified in part (i).

On $\{X_\infty<\infty,\ A_\infty=\infty\}$ we would have $M_n\to-\infty$. On $\{X_\infty=\infty,\ A_\infty<\infty\}$ we would have $M_n\to+\infty$. Both <events> have <probability> zero by the convergence-or-oscillation conclusion. Hence the two divergence <events> agree up to a <null set>. Since $X_\infty=\infty$ means precisely that infinitely many $B_n$ occur,
$$
\boxed{\{B_n\text{ infinitely often}\}=\left\{\sum_{k=1}^\infty\mathbb E[\mathbf1_{B_k}\mid\mathcal F_{k-1}]=\infty\right\}\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
Taking <probabilities> proves the displayed equality in the question. This stronger agreement of <events> is the <conditional Borel-Cantelli lemma>.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Suppose the <events> $(B_n)$ are mutually <independent events>. Then $B_k$ is <independent> of $\mathcal F_{k-1}$, first by factorization against finite <intersections> of previous <events> and then by part (a). Therefore
$$
\mathbb E[\mathbf1_{B_k}\mid\mathcal F_{k-1}]=\mathbb P(B_k)\quad\text{almost surely}.
$$
If $\sum_k\mathbb P(B_k)=\infty$, the sum in part (ii) is infinite <almost surely>, and consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_n\text{ infinitely often})=1.}
$$
This is the <second Borel-Cantelli lemma>. In fact part (ii) also yields <probability> zero in the convergent case for these <independent events>.

